 "note": "[agent-bulk] numucc_em with a reco pi0. drag_check both true on this capture. shw 15009 (130.5 MeV, 10 seg, conn 2, pio_id 0) is a compact single-apex object: its cluster-15 body runs 0-21.8 cm at 0.0-14.3 deg on both azimuths, and its acceptance plot carries NO non-member circle anywhere in the window out to 120 cm and 18 deg, so there is nothing contiguous left unclaimed -- the only unowned thing inside 40 cm is 56026, a 0.2 cm stub at 34.6 cm/35.8 deg, and everything else in the event is at >=98 deg. The one thing I would not defend hard: three of the ten members are sub-centimetre pass4_angle stubs far down the axis -- 51021 (0.3 cm) at 37.6 cm/4.9 deg, 52022 (0.3 cm) at 59.1 cm/8.6 deg, 53023 (0.4 cm) at 92.7 cm/4.8 deg. For a 130 MeV shower with a 22 cm body, reaching 93 cm is a stretch, but they are on-axis, individually spaced along it rather than a clump, and carry no meaningful charge, so I did not mark them; this is NOT the evt282979 pattern (there the members jumped an empty 14-95 cm band into a dense off-axis clump). Vertex: the star is where the 153.7 cm muon (9000) and the 108.8 cm pdg-2212 track (16017) meet, both correctly unowned. pi0: the reco's group 0 (15009 + 60031) is confirmed and stored -- the pi0 panel shows the two gammas leaving the vertex in near-opposite directions and the vertex convention gives 141.9 MeV (axis convention 157.3), so the vertex-convention answer is within 7 MeV of 135. Not judged individually: 16016 (78.3 MeV but pdg 2212 and only 3.7 cm) and 61033 (50.5 MeV, 1.0 cm, pdg 2212) are range-converted proton stubs, not EM showers; shw 60031 (55.5 MeV, 2 seg) I only checked for the pairing. 9 further showers skipped by the scope rule, all under 20 MeV.",
